# --explain shows a pack rule's words and names the pack it comes from
spec: Packs; CLI
args: --explain rust-style/weasel-type-names
-- necro.yaml --
version: 0
include: [necro:rust-style]
-- @stdout --
rust-style/weasel-type-names: a type named for a role, not a thing

Why: Manager, Service, Helper and the like say a type exists, not what it holds or does, so it becomes the place every unrelated function lands.

Fix: name what it is (`Pool`, `Cache`, `Renderer`), or split it; a builder is fine as `…Builder`.

From Microsoft's Pragmatic Rust Guidelines (M-WEASEL-WORDS).
Experimental: no hits to tune against yet.

Bad:
    struct ConfigManager {

Good:
    struct Config {

Command: necro grep '\b(struct|enum|trait|type)\s+\w*({role-words})\b' -- '*.rs'
Files: \.rs$
Param role-words: Manager|Service|Factory|Helpers?|Utils?|Handler
Pack: necro:rust-style, built into necro <version>.
